{
  "root_cause": "Pikaday's global keydown listener processes ArrowLeft (and other arrow keys) whenever the calendar is visible, regardless of which element has focus. This prevents the default caret movement in other inputs, causing the space to be inserted at the end instead of between characters.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify _onKeyChange to early‑return when the calendar is visible but neither the associated field nor the picker container contains the active element. This preserves datepicker keyboard navigation when focus is inside the field or the calendar, while allowing normal editing behavior elsewhere.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "pikaday.js",
      "sha256": "662123ec9f429dee9cffaacfcf89b05ed9c3c30a78d33ecfd113e6778349cc77",
      "before": "        self._onKeyChange = function(e)
        {
            e = e || window.event;

            if (self.isVisible()) {

                switch(e.keyCode){
                    case 13:
                    case 27:
                        if (opts.field) {
                            opts.field.blur();
                        }
                        break;
                    case 37:
                        e.preventDefault();
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 1);
                        break;
                    case 38:
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 7);
                        break;
                    case 39:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 1);
                        break;
                    case 40:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 7);
                        break;
                }
            }
        };",
      "after": "        self._onKeyChange = function(e)
        {
            e = e || window.event;

            if (self.isVisible()) {
                if (opts.field !== document.activeElement && !self.el.contains(document.activeElement)) {
                    return;
                }

                switch(e.keyCode){
                    case 13:
                    case 27:
                        if (opts.field) {
                            opts.field.blur();
                        }
                        break;
                    case 37:
                        e.preventDefault();
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 1);
                        break;
                    case 38:
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 7);
                        break;
                    case 39:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 1);
                        break;
                    case 40:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 7);
                        break;
                }
            }
        };"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "ArrowLeft and ArrowRight will now move the caret in text inputs when the datepicker calendar is visible, enabling users to correct typos without the datepicker interfering.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "If a user focuses an element outside the field and picker while the calendar is open, arrow keys will no longer adjust the date (they previously would have). This is the intended behavior.",
    "If opts.field is null and the picker is container‑only, reliance on self.el.contains ensures arrow keys work only when focus is inside the calendar."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Verify that ArrowLeft in the #notes input moves the caret left when the calendar is visible.",
    "Verify that ArrowLeft/Right/Up/Dow​n still change the selected date when focus is inside the calendar or the delivery-date field.",
    "Verify that Enter and Esc still close the picker when focus is inside the field or calendar.",
    "Verify that typing in other inputs is not affected by the calendar's visibility."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Journey steps:
1. seek selector "#notes"
2. type "Gate4"
3. press ArrowLeft
4. type " "

Assertion after step 4 expects notes field value ["Gate 4"] but observed was ["Gate4 "] meaning after typing Gate4 then pressing ArrowLeft then space resulted in "Gate4 " instead of "Gate 4". So the issue: When focusing on the notes input, pressing ArrowLeft moved the caret? Actually we typed Gate4, then pressed ArrowLeft (which likely moved caret left within the input?), then typed space resulting in insertion at caret position leading to "Gate4 ". But expected outcome: After moving left, inserting space between t and 4? Wait sequence: Starting empty, type G a t e 4 -> "Gate4". Then press ArrowLeft (move caret left one character, so caret positioned between 't' and '4'?). Actually after typing Gate4, caret is at end (after 4). Press ArrowLeft moves caret left one position, so between 'e' and '4'? Let's think: characters indices: 0 G,1 a,2 t,3 e,4 4. Caret after typing is at index 5 (after char 4). ArrowLeft moves caret to index 4 (before the '4'). Then typing space inserts space at index 4, resulting "Gate 4"? Actually inserting space before the '4' yields "Gate 4". That matches expectation. However observed result was "Gate4 " meaning space appended at end, implying ArrowLeft didn't move caret; maybe it triggered something else like calendar navigation? Indeed the component pikaday.js may be capturing keydown events globally (document) for arrow keys to adjust date when calendar is visible. If calendar is visible (maybe because the delivery-date input has focus earlier? Actually journey started with seek #notes, but before that maybe focus was on delivery-date? Not sure). In observed keyEvents list: we see sequence of keydown events: Tab from body to #delivery-date, then Tab to #notes, then G,a,t,e,4 keydown on #notes, then ArrowLeft keydown on #notes with defaultPrevented:true, then space keydown on #notes with defaultPrevented:false. So ArrowLeft was prevented default (prevented) meaning pikaday's _onKeyChange prevented default, causing caret not to move, and then space inserted at end. So the bug: pikaday's global keydown listener (on document) is interfering with ArrowLeft in any input when the pikaday calendar is bound? Actually pikaday adds keydown listener to document if keyboardInput:true (default true). It checks if self.isVisible() then handles arrow keys to adjust date. However in our scenario, the calendar might be visible? The delivery-date field has a pikaday attached, bound:false, container: #calendar. The calendar is rendered in #calendar div. Is it visible? Probably yes because container is set and bound:false leads to show() on init? In pikaday constructor: if opts.bound then hide initially else show(). Since bound:false, it calls this.show(); So calendar is visible when page loads. Therefore self.isVisible() returns true, and keydown listener intercepts ArrowLeft etc., preventing default and adjusting date (adjustDate subtract 1). That prevents caret movement in notes input.

Thus fix: pikaday should only respond to arrow keys when the calendar is visible AND the active element is the field it's bound to (or maybe when the picker itself has focus). According to typical behavior, datepicker keyboard navigation should work when the picker is focused, not when any other input has focus. The pikaday currently adds listener to document and checks self.isVisible() only. Need to also check that the event target is either the field or something inside the picker (like the table). Or we could restrict keydown listener to only when the picker has focus (i.e., when the picker element is activeElement or contains activeElement). Simpler: modify _onKeyChange to return early if !(opts.field && document.activeElement === opts.field) && !self.el.contains(document.activeElement). Actually we want to allow arrow keys when focus is inside the picker (the calendar) or the field (if bound). For unbound case with container, we want arrow keys to work when calendar has focus (user can navigate via tab into calendar). So condition: if (self.isVisible() && (opts.field === document.activeElement || self.el.contains(document.activeElement))) then process keys; else ignore.

